4. Why the split is where it is

   The open core is a complete, useful governance layer on its own: policy
   enforcement, a tamper-evident audit ledger, per-run cost caps, and a
   model-agnostic runtime. Anyone can run it, and the CLI's core commands work
   without the commercial modules installed.

   The commercial modules are the ones a business buys rather than builds:
   the policy packs and their self-check, cost and integrity analytics,
   policy-gated secret leases, the SOC 2 / ISO 27001 control mapping, the
   dashboard, and the framework adapters.

   Both halves are in one repository because a buyer evaluating AXON needs to
   read all of it. The license boundary, not the repository boundary, is what
   makes the difference.
